<wpf:ResourceDictionary xml:space="preserve" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml" xmlns:s="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib" xmlns:ss="urn:shemas-jetbrains-com:settings-storage-xaml" xmlns:wpf="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation">
	<!--
		MAUI's XAML source generator emits two files per .xaml page, App.xaml.sg.cs and
		App.xaml.xsg.cs, and ReSharper resolves the pair inconsistently: the half that implements
		InitializeComponent cannot see the half that declares it. That produces "No defining
		declaration found", "Cannot resolve symbol 'InitializeComponentRuntime'" and complaints
		about __XamlGeneratedCode__ types across every page. The compiler has no such trouble -
		dotnet build is clean - so these are ReSharper's own resolve errors on generated code.

		Skipped here rather than marked as generated code on purpose: for generated code ReSharper
		still runs the inspections that report compiler errors, which is exactly what these are, so
		a GeneratedFileMasks entry would leave all 84 of them on screen. Ignored Code is the only
		switch that covers them, and two masks cover every page at once.

		These are masks in ReSharper | Options | Code Inspection | Ignored Code. Both halves are
		listed because *.sg.cs does not match *.xsg.cs - the character before "sg.cs" is an "x",
		not a dot.
	-->
	<s:Boolean x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/ExcludedFiles/FileMasksToSkip/=_002A_002Exsg_002Ecs/@EntryIndexedValue">True</s:Boolean>
	<s:Boolean x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/ExcludedFiles/FileMasksToSkip/=_002A_002Esg_002Ecs/@EntryIndexedValue">True</s:Boolean>
</wpf:ResourceDictionary>